    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_c06ab5e9e0285d29a038b13222c6187e19c98402e0fed24b3eb51bd9974f2f63",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The prime-orbit dictionary fixes primitive periods as log p and therefore fixes log 2 as the shortest period. At the distinct long-orbit scale needed to resolve mean-spaced levels, orbit proliferation cancels squared-amplitude decay in the Hannay-Ozorio sum rule and removes system detail; at the short-orbit scale, failure of that sum rule restores the log 2 datum and predicts the growing separation at which Riemann-zero statistics depart from GUE. The same period assignment thus determines both the local universal regime and its nonuniform boundary.",
